# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.3 have "¬pleasure(virtue) ∧ ¬pain(virtue) → ¬foritself(virtue)"; mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.2 has "¬pleasure(virtue) ∧ ¬pain(virtue) → ¬desired(virtue) ∨ means(virtue) ∧ ¬foritself(virtue)"; they differ when only "virtue is desired" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "virtue is desired" is true and "virtue is desired as a means to some end beyond itself, and ultimately to happiness", "virtue is desired for its own sake", "the consciousness of being without virtue is a pain" and "the consciousness of virtue is a pleasure" are false. Is S4, "If one of these gave him no pleasure, and the other no pain, he would not love or desire virtue, or would desire it only for the other benefits which it might produce to himself or to persons whom he cared for.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1 has "pleasure(virtue) ∨ pain(virtue) → part(virtue)" and "∀x (means(x) ∨ part(x) → onlyhappiness(x))"; mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.2 and mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.3 have "∀x (means(x) ∨ part(x) → onlyhappiness(x))"; they differ when only "virtue is desired as a means to some end beyond itself, and ultimately to happiness", "the consciousness of being without virtue is a pain", "the consciousness of virtue is a pleasure" and "virtue is desired for nothing except happiness" are true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1 takes for granted "if (either the consciousness of virtue is a pleasure or the consciousness of being without virtue is a pain), then virtue is desired as itself a part of happiness"; mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.2 and mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1.3 do not
mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (either the consciousness of virtue is a pleasure or the consciousness of being without virtue is a pain), then virtue is desired as itself a part of happiness"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
mill-utilitarianism-2.md:1: argue/quantifier: mill-utilitarianism-2.logic.r1 P1 (S2): the sentence says "some", but P1 says all: it reads "for every x, if (x is desired and it is not so that x is desired as a means to some end beyond itself, and ultimately to happiness), then x is desired as itself a part of happiness"
